{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n \"accepted.limit|accepted_limits|within-limit|WithinLimit|reviewFindingDecisionBlockers|hasAcceptedLimitReason|ParseAcceptedLimits\" pose-mcp/internal/pose pose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go POSE.md locales/pt-BR/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md .pose/knowledge'","aggregated_output":"pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md:1065:**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit; `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\nlocales/pt-BR/POSE.md:1075:**Limites aceitos.** Uma spec pode declarar os limites residuais das suas garantias em `### Accepted limits`, um `- L<N>: <suposição>. Accepted because <motivo>.` por limite; `lint-spec --ready-check` recusa um limite que não diz por que é aceito e, quando um ADR cita a spec, um que o ADR não nomeia. O plano de review e o bundle selam os limites (`accepted_limits`, parte do digest do plano; num escopo com várias specs cada id é qualificado como `<spec>#L<N>`), e o brief do template `review-brief/11` os lista: um achado que cai dentro de um deles é um follow-up com `limit` igual ao seu id, registrado na atestação com a disposição `within-limit`, que não bloqueia uma aprovação seja qual for a severidade; um limite que o bundle não selou, ou um achado `within-limit` sem motivo, é recusado. Um revisor que ache um limite injustificado relata um achado que o contesta, que bloqueia como qualquer achado. Uma review converge classificando o que foi declarado fora do escopo, não por um teto de rodadas.\nPOSE.md:1078:**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit; `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\npose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go:574:\t\tlimits, limitProblems := posepkg.ParseAcceptedLimits(text)\npose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go:1198:\t\treturn []string{\"DoR: the ADRs cannot be read to check the accepted limits: \" + err.Error()}\npose-mcp/internal/cli/lintspec.go:1208:\t\t\tunreadable = append(unreadable, \"DoR: ADR \"+e.Name()+\" cannot be read to check the accepted limits: \"+err.Error())\npose-